 yields 320 corn in six months.\n\n4. **Add up the total yield from both fields:**\n   - Johnson's total yield over six months is 240 corn.\n   - His neighbor's total yield over six months is 320 corn.\n   - Adding these together gives:\n     \\[\n     240 + 320 = 560 \\text{ corn}\n     \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nTogether, Johnson and his neighbor can harvest a total of 560 corn after six months.", "To determine how many years Devin has taught, we need to calculate the number of years he spent teaching each subject and then sum these amounts.\n\n1. **Calculus:**\n   Devin taught Calculus for 4 years.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Years teaching Calculus} = 4\n   \\]\n\n2. **Algebra:**\n   Devin taught Algebra for twice as many years as he did Calculus.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Years teaching Algebra} = 2 \\times 4 = 8\n   \\]\n\n3. **Statistics:**\n   Devin taught Statistics for 5 times as long as he taught Algebra.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Years teaching Statistics} = 5 \\times 8 = 40\n   \\]\n\nNow, let's add up all the years:\n\\[\n\\text{Total years taught} = \\text{Years teaching Calculus} + \\text{Years teaching Algebra} + \\text{Years teaching Statistics}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Total years taught} = 4 + 8 + 40 = 52\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Devin has taught for a total of 52 years. \n\n#### 52", "Step 1: Calculate the total number of cherries needed.\nVeronica needs 3 pounds of pitted cherries.\nThere are 80 cherries in one pound.\nTotal cherries = 3 pounds * 80 cherries/pound\n\nStep 2: Calculate the time required to pit all the cherries.\nIt takes 10 minutes to pit 20 cherries.\nFirst, find out how many groups of 20 cherries there are in the total number of cherries:\nGroups of 20 cherries = Total cherries / 20 cherries/group\n\nStep 3: Convert the time from minutes to hours.\nSince there are 60 minutes in an hour, divide the total minutes by 60 to get the time in hours.\n\nLet's do the calculations:\n\nStep 1: \nTotal cherries = 3 * 80 = 240 cherries\n\nStep 2: \nGroups of 20 cherries = 240 c