spend $30.00 on berries in a 30-day period.\n\n#### 30.00", "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\nStep 1: Convert gallons to cups.\nThere are 16 cups in one gallon. So, for 4.5 gallons:\n\\[ 4.5 \\text{ gallons} \\times 16 \\text{ cups/gallon} = 72 \\text{ cups} \\]\n\nStep 2: Divide the total number of cups by the number of thermoses.\nWe have 72 cups and 18 thermoses:\n\\[ 72 \\text{ cups} \u00f7 18 \\text{ thermoses} = 4 \\text{ cups/thermos} \\]\n\nStep 3: Calculate how much coffee Genevieve drank.\nGenevieve drank 3 thermoses, so:\n\\[ 3 \\text{ thermoses} \u00d7 4 \\text{ cups/thermos} = 12 \\text{ cups} \\]\n\nStep 4: Convert cups to pints.\nThere are 2 cups in one pint. So, for 12 cups:\n\\[ 12 \\text{ cups} \u00f7 2 \\text{ cups/pint} = 6 \\text{ pints} \\]\n\nTherefore, Genevieve drank 6 pints of coffee.\n\n#### 6", "To solve this problem, we need to determine how long it takes for the dish to reach 100 degrees from its initial temperature of 20 degrees, given that it increases by 5 degrees per minute.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the total increase needed.\nThe dish needs to go from 20 degrees to 100 degrees. So, the total increase required is:\n\\[ \\text{Total Increase} = 100 - 20 = 80 \\text{ degrees} \\]\n\nStep 2: Determine the rate of heating.\nThe dish heats up by 5 degrees each minute.\n\nStep 3: Calculate the time required to achieve the total increase.\nSince the dish heats up by 5 degrees per minute, the number of minutes required can be calculated as follows:\n\\[ \\text{Time Required} = \\frac{\\text{Total Increase}}{\\text{Rate of Heating}} = \\frac{80}{5} = 16 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nTherefore, it will take Jenny **16** minutes to heat the dish to the desired temperature.", "To determine how many dozens of eggs Milo should buy to get 6 pounds of eggs, we can follow these steps:\n\nStep 1: Calculate the weight of one egg.\nAn egg weighs \\( \\frac{1}{16} \\) of a pound.\n\nStep 2: Determine how many eggs are needed to make 6 pounds of eggs.\nSince each egg weighs \\( \\frac{1}{16} \\) of a pound, we divide 6 pounds by the weight of one egg:\n\\[ \\text{Number of eggs} = \\frac{6 \\text{ pounds}}{\\frac{1}{16} \\text{ pounds per egg}} = 6 \\times 16 = 96 \\text{ eggs} \\]\n\nStep 3: Convert the number of eggs into dozens.\nThere are 12 eggs in a dozen, so we divide the number of eggs by 12:\n\\[ \\text{Number of dozens} = \\frac{96 \\text{ eggs}}{12 \\text{ eggs per dozen}} = 8 \\text{ dozens} \\]\n\nTherefore, Milo should buy 8 dozen eggs.\n\n#### 8", "To determine how much time Carson will spend gardening, we need to calculate the total time spent on both tasks: mowing the lawn and planting flowers.\n\n1. **Mowing the Lawn:**\n   - Number of lines to be mowed: 40\n   - Time taken to mow one line: 2 minutes\n\n   Total time for mowing = Number of lines \u00d7 Time per line\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total time for mowing} = 40 \\times 2 = 80 \\text{ minutes}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Planting Flowers:**\n   - Number of rows of flowers: 8\n   - Number of flowers in each row: 7\n   - Time taken to plant one flower: 0.5 minutes\n\n   First, calculate the total number of flowers:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of flowers} = 8 \\times 7 = 56\n   \\]\n\n   Then, calculate the total time for planting all the flowers:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total time for planting} = 56 \\times 0.5 = 28 \\text{ minutes}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Total Gardening Time:**\n   Add the time spent on mowing and planting:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total gardening time} = 80 + 28 = 108 \\text{ minutes}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total time Carson will spend gardening is \\( \\boxed{108} \\) minutes.", "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the number of games each person won and then sum these numbers.\n\n1. **Betsy's Wins:**\n   - Betsy won 5 games.\n   \n2. **Helen's Wins:**\n   - Helen won twice as many games as Betsy.\n     \\[\n     \\text{Helen's wins} = 2 \\times 5 = 10\n     \\]\n\n3. **Susan's Wins:**\n   - Susan won three times as many games as 