s Jaylen has:**\n   - Carrots: 5\n   - Cucumbers: 2\n   - Bell peppers: 4\n   - Green beans: 7\n\nNow, add all these together to find the total number of vegetables Jaylen has:\n\n\\[\n5 + 2 + 4 + 7 = 18\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of vegetables Jaylen has is \\( \\boxed{18} \\).", "To determine how long it will take for Janet to fertilize all her fields, we need to break down the problem into smaller steps:\n\n1. **Calculate total fertilizer needed:**\n   - Each acre requires 400 gallons of fertilizer.\n   - Janet has 20 acres of farmland.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total fertilizer needed} = 20 \\text{ acres} \\times 400 \\text{ gallons/acre} = 8000 \\text{ gallons}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine daily production rate:**\n   - Each horse produces 5 gallons of fertilizer per day.\n   - Janet has 80 horses.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Daily production} = 80 \\text{ horses} \\times 5 \\text{ gallons/horse/day} = 400 \\text{ gallons/day}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the number of days required to produce the total amount of fertilizer:**\n   - Total fertilizer needed is 8000 gallons.\n   - Daily production is 400 gallons.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of days} = \\frac{\\text{Total fertilizer needed}}{\\text{Daily production}} = \\frac{8000 \\text{ gallons}}{400 \\text{ gallons/day}} = 20 \\text{ days}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Verify the spreading capacity:**\n   - Janet can spread fertilizer over 4 acres per day.\n   - She has 20 acres to be fertilized.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Days to spread fertilizer} = \\frac{\\text{Total acres}}{\\text{Acres per day}} = \\frac{20 \\text{ acres}}{4 \\text{ acres/day}} = 5 \\text{ days}\n   \\]\n   \n", "To solve this problem, we need to determine how long it takes for Jim to catch up to Bob after he starts running. Let's break down the steps:\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the relative speed between Jim and Bob.\nJim runs at 9 mph (miles per hour), while Bob runs at 6 mph. The difference in their speeds is:\n\\[ \\text{Relative Speed} = 9 - 6 = 3 \\text{ mph} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Determine the distance Bob needs to cover to be caught.\nSince Bob has a 1-mile head start, we need to find out how much time it takes for Jim to close that gap.\n\n### Step 3: Use the formula \\( \\text{Distance} = \\text{Speed} \\times \\text{Time} \\).\nWe know the relative speed (\\(3\\) mph) and the initial distance (\\(1\\) mile). We can rearrange the formula to solve for time:\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Distance}}{\\text{Speed}} \\]\nSubstituting the known values:\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{3 \\text{ mph}} \\]\n\n### Step 4: Convert the time from hours to minutes.\nThere are 60 minutes in an hour, so we convert the time:\n\\[ \\text{Time in minutes} = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\times 60 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Time in minutes} = 20 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nTherefore, it will take Jim **20** minutes to catch up to Bob.", "To determine which fisherman catches more fish, we'll calculate the total number of fish each catches over the 213-day period.\n\n### Fisherman 1:\n- Catches 3 fish/day.\n- Total days: 213 days.\n- Total fish caught = \\( 3 \\times 213 = 639 \\) fish.\n\n### Fisherman 2:\n- First 30 days: 1 fish/day.\n- Next 60 days: 2 fish/day.\n- Remaining days (213 - 30 - 60): 123 days.\n- Total fish caught in the first 30 days = \\( 1 \\times 30 = 30 \\) fish.\n- Total fish caught in the next 60 days = \\( 2 \\times 60 = 120 \\) fish.\n- Total fish caught in the remaining 123 days = \\( 4 \\times 123 = 492 \\) fish.\n- Total fish caught = \\( 30 + 120 + 492 = 642 \\) fish.\n\n### Difference in Fish Caught:\n- Fisherman 2 caught \\( 642 - 639 = 3 \\) more fish than Fisherman 1.\n\nTherefore, the fisherman who catches the higher number of fish ends up catching **3** more fish.", "To determine how much of the budget remains after purchasing flasks, test tubes, and safety gear, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the cost of test tubes:**\n   - The lab spent $150 on flasks.\n   - They spent two-thirds of this amount on test tubes.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Cost of test tubes} = \\frac{2}{3} \\times 150 = 100 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the cost of safety gear:**\n   - They spent half of the cost of test tubes on safety gear.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Cost of safety gear} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 100 = 50 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sum up all the expenses:**\n   - Cost of flasks: $150\n   - Cost of test tubes: $100\n   - Cost of safety gear: $50\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total expenses} = 150 + 100 + 50 = 300 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the remaining budget:**\n   - Initial budget: $325\n   - Total expenses: $300\n   \\[\n   \\text{Remaining budget} = 325 - 300 = 25 \\text{ dollars}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of dollars of the budget that remained is:\n#### 25", "To solve this problem, let's break it down into steps:\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the total number of dancers.\n- There were 140 kids in total at the junior prom.\n- A fourth of these kids were dancers.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of dancers} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 140 = 35\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Determine how many dancers did not slow dance.\n- We know that 25 dancers participated in the slow dance.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Dancers who did not slow dance} = \\text{Total dancers} - \\text{Dancers who slowed}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Dancers who did not slow dance} = 35 - 25 = 10\n\\]\n\n### Final Answer:\nThe number of dancer students who did not slow dance is **10**.", "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the total savings over four weeks since there are typically four weeks in a month.\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n### Week 1:\n- Jay starts by saving $20.\n- Increase: +$10\n\nTotal for Week 1 = $20 (initial) + $10 (increase) = **$30**\n\n### Week 2:\n- Jay saves an additional $10 compared to the previous week.\n- Total for Week 2 = $30 (previous week) + $10 (increase) = **$40**\n\n### Week 3:\n- Jay saves another $10 more than the previous week.\n- Total for Week 3 = $40 (previous week) + $10 (increase) = **$50**\n\n### Week 4:\n- Jay saves one last time before the end of the month.\n- Total for Week 4 = $50 (previous week) + $10 (increase) = **$60**\n\nNow, let's sum up all the weekly totals to find out how much Jay will have saved in a month from now:\n\nTotal Savings = $30 (Week 1) + $40 (Week 2) + $50 (Week 3) + $60 (Week 4)\n\nTotal Savings = **$180**\n\nTherefore, after four weeks, or in a month from now, Jay will have saved **$180**.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of each type of cat that each person has and then sum them up.\n\n1. **Jamie's Cats:**\n   - Jamie owns 4 Persian cats.\n   - Jamie owns 2 Maine Coons.\n\n2. **Gordon's Cats:**\n   - Gordon owns half as many Persians as Jamie, so he has \\( \\frac{4}{2} = 2 \\) Persian cats.\n   - Gordon owns one more Maine Coon than Jamie, so he has \\( 2 + 1 = 3 \\) Maine Coons.\n\n3. **Hawkeye's Cats:**\n   - Hawkeye owns no Persian cats.\n   - Hawkeye owns one less Maine Coon than Gordon, so he has \\( 3 - 1 = 2 \\) Maine Coons.\n\nNow, let's add up all the cats:\n\n- Jamie's cats: \\( 4 \\) Persian + \\( 2 \\) Maine Coons = \\( 6 \\)\n- Gordon's cats: \\( 2 \\) Persian + \\( 3 \\) Maine Coons = \\( 5 \\)\n- Hawkeye's cats: \\( 0 \\) Persian + \\( 2 \\) Maine Coons = \\( 2 \\)\n\nTotal number of cats:\n\\[ 6 \\text{ (Jamie)} + 5 \\text{ (Gordon)} + 2 \\text{ (Hawkeye)} = 13 \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of cats when brought together is \\(\\boxed{13}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Determine the initial number of black cookies.\n2. Calculate the number of black cookies eaten.\n3. Calculate the number of white cookies eaten.\n4. Find out how many cookies remain after eating some.\n5. Sum up the remaining cookies from both types.\n\nStep 1: Initial number of black cookies\nSince Cristian has 50 more black cookies than white cookies, and he initially had 80 white cookies:\n\\[ \\text{Initial number of black cookies} = 80 + 50 = 130 \\]\n\nStep 2: Number of black cooki